a beach littered with plastic spat out  by a sea that can't take much more  it's a sight that's come to symbolize  the scale of plastic pollution in Mumbai  where waste management systems continue  to fall short but these residents of the  city are trying to do their bit for  close to three years they've gathered  here on weekends to clean up this beach  their leader afro Shah is a lawyer by  training not an environmentalist but  after moving into the neighborhood he  grew tired of seeing the state of the  environment around him and took matters  into his own hands  for me I have to connect with the needs  what I've taken into the ocean till date  I do not shout out to people to come I  tell people come by choice why should  anybody shout out to protect their money  you should feel there's a problem and I  can give two hours in a week Feroz  energy Shaw's approach has slowly  gathered steam the movement is now in  its a hundred and forty second week and  has attracted volunteers from all walks  of life it's also offered employment to  about 35 workers who keep efforts going  during the week afros Shah says his  beach cleanups have cleared around  15,000 tons of plastic but it's only  just beginning to scratch the surface of  the problem activists now hope a recent  government ban on single-use plastics  here in Mumbai will help further change  mindsets the city governments  anti-plastic squads are key to the  effort clad in lead vests the squad's  work in markets throughout the city to  enforce the new bands and raise  awareness about plastic pollution  there's a lot of confusion here about  what's banned and what's not but the  anti-plastic Squad is looking to make it  clear one-time use carry bags are a  particular focus of attention some  shopkeepers and customers are heeding  the call shifting to bags made out of  cloth or jus other shopkeepers should  stop using plastic and spend a little  more money for cloth bags they are much  better and reusable plastic is harmful  for everyone but not everyone is on  board vendors at this market say the ban  is hitting their bottom line and that  plastic bags are the only viable option  during the ongoing monsoon season a  customer who buys five lemons can put  them in his pocket but if he buys 50 he  can't carry them in his hands he needs a  bag with cloth bags if he buys 10 lemons  for 10 rupees it doesn't work for us to  give him a bag worth 2 or 3 rupees since  the banned customers have not been  buying vegetables from us they won't  take it wrapped in paper customers want  bags they say their stuff gets wet in  the rain and spoiled they love and are  mainly loved ocean but they now have to  get that's temperature to a fresh ah the  resistance the plastic ban is faced  isn't a surprise  he knows the mindset changes required to  get more people off single-use plastic  will take time until then he and his  team are moving their campaign beyond  the beach to businesses across the city  due to their efforts this restaurant has  invested in about 20 of these reusable  bags for food deliveries  shai is confident more will soon follow  suit and in doing so will also help  deliver a cleaner future for Mumbai
 

